{"id": "own3-neg-unknown-region", "rules": ["OWN-3"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-3 negative: a borrow names region 'z that was never introduced (no region_params, no enclosing region 'z block) \u2014 rejected citing OWN-3."}
{"id": "own4-pos-return-caller-borrow", "rules": ["OWN-4", "OWN-14"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-4 positive: a borrow made in caller-supplied region 'r0 is returned as rtype region 'r0 \u2014 the returned region equals the borrow's region, so the escape is legal; accepted."}
{"id": "own4-neg-return-local-borrow", "rules": ["OWN-4", "OWN-14"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-4 negative: a borrow made in local region 's is returned; a local-region borrow does not outlive the caller and escapes via return \u2014 rejected citing OWN-4."}
{"id": "own5-pos-read-through-holder", "rules": ["OWN-5"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-5 positive: while a &uniq borrow is live, reads and writes through that borrow's holder are the permitted access path; write-then-read through u, then read the place after the region closes. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "own5-neg-read-while-uniq", "rules": ["OWN-5"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-5 negative: while a &uniq borrow of a is live, reading a directly (not through the holder) is an overlapping access — rejected citing OWN-5."}
{"id": "own6-pos-callscoped-temp", "rules": ["OWN-6"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "reason": "OWN-6 (holder/resolution/temporary lifetime) is the mechanism OWN-5/OWN-7 judge over; it is never separately cited, so only a positive that exercises the call-scoped-temporary clause is authored.", "doc": "OWN-6 positive: a borrow passed as a call argument is an unbound call-scoped temporary that dies at the end of its statement; two successive &uniq-argument calls over the same place therefore do not conflict — accepted."}
{"id": "own7-pos-distinct-noverlap", "rules": ["OWN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "reason": "OWN-7 defines overlap but is not cited alone; an overlap violation cites OWN-5 or OWN-12. This positive exercises the distinct-root non-overlap case, while field, index, and slice cases belong to the corresponding semantic families.", "doc": "OWN-7 positive: resolved places rooted at distinct bindings a and b do not overlap, so two live &uniq borrows coexist and each mutates its own root; runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "own10-pos-local-region", "rules": ["OWN-10"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-10 positive: an own binding a is borrowed into a local region 'r introduced within a's scope; storage outlives the borrow, so it is legal. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "own10-neg-dangle-caller", "rules": ["OWN-10"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-10"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-10 negative: borrowing an own parameter x into caller-supplied region 'r0 (which outlives the frame) would dangle after return \u2014 rejected citing OWN-10 before codegen."}
{"id": "own11-pos-loop-inner-region", "rules": ["OWN-11"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-11 positive: inside loop @l a borrow names region 's introduced within @l's body (reading an outer binding by copy is exempt); one iteration then break. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "own11-neg-borrow-outer-region", "rules": ["OWN-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-11"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-11 negative (first clause): inside loop @l a borrow names region 'r introduced outside the loop \u2014 rejected citing OWN-11 (a cross-iteration borrow would alias itself)."}
{"id": "own11-neg-move-outer-in-loop", "rules": ["OWN-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-11"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-11 negative (second clause): a binding s declared outside loop @l is moved inside it (via an own-scrutinee match); a second iteration would use a dead binding \u2014 rejected citing OWN-11."}
{"id": "own12-pos-distinct-uniq-args", "rules": ["OWN-12"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-12 positive: two &uniq arguments to one call whose resolved places (x and y) are distinct do not alias — accepted."}
{"id": "own12-neg-alias-uniq-args", "rules": ["OWN-12"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-12"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-12 negative: two &uniq arguments to the same call whose resolved places overlap (both borrow x) — rejected citing OWN-12."}
{"id": "own13-pos-let-match-give", "rules": ["OWN-13"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-13 positive (let-initializer match): a value-match scrutinee is an owned temporary; each arm delivers the let's declared own value via give, and on the taken arm the own result is moved exactly once (no double-move, T1). Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "own13-pos-borrow-match-live", "rules": ["OWN-13"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-13 positive (borrow scrutinee): matching through &'r leaves the scrutinee live (binder modes derived, not written); s is matched a second time after the first match \u2014 accepted, proving the borrow-mode match did not move it."}
{"id": "stor1-pos-frame-resident", "rules": ["STOR-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Storage class is a function of type [STOR-1]: an i32 local is frame-resident, with no per-binding storage annotation and no default clause; runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "stor2-pos-box-new", "rules": ["STOR-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Creation: box_new::<T>(v) returns own box<T> [STOR-2]; content is read through deref."}
{"id": "stor3-pos-box-drop-region", "rules": ["STOR-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A box allocated in a region is freed by a compiler-derived drop on every region-exit edge, in reverse declaration order [STOR-3]; the drop is explicit in the checked program."}
{"id": "stor4-pos-arena-confined", "rules": ["STOR-4"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An arena<'r,T> stays within its region 'r, its content is read through deref, and it is released at 'r's block exit [STOR-4]."}
{"id": "stor4-neg-arena-escape", "rules": ["STOR-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "STOR-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A value of type arena<'r,T> may not be returned, stored into a field, or moved outside 'r's block [STOR-4]; returning it escapes the region and must be rejected."}
{"id": "stor4-neg-borrowed-arena-result", "rules": ["STOR-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "STOR-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Arena confinement reads mode and type together: a result whose type is arena<'r,T> may not leave 'r's block in any result mode, so a borrow-mode arena result is the same escape as an owned one. The declaration is the error and no call is required to reach it, and STOR-4 is the rule defined first among those established at the complete rtype, so it is cited there rather than FN-1's borrow-result provenance judgment, which applies to a region-free result type."}
{"id": "op1-pos-table-op", "rules": ["OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Every computation is a call naming exactly one operation from the operation table [OP-1]; nothing is overloaded. A wrapping add and an integer compare run to exit 0."}
{"id": "op1-neg-ineg-unsigned", "rules": ["OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ineg has exactly one signed integer type argument [OP-1]; an unsigned instantiation is rejected rather than inferred or reinterpreted."}
{"id": "op2-pos-div-checked", "rules": ["OP-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "idiv.checked yields Result<T, DivError> [OP-2]: Err(DivideByZero()) for a zero divisor, Err(DivOverflow()) for signed iK::MIN/-1, else Ok."}
{"id": "op2-pos-ineg-modes", "rules": ["OP-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The exact, wrapping, and checked negation families run through one program [OP-2]: `ineg.wrap` keeps minimum at minimum, dotless exact `ineg` admits a representable negation through its `ineg.defined` obligation, and `ineg.checked` returns Err(Overflow()) for minimum."}
{"id": "op2-pos-guarded-subtraction-l0-bridge", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-3", "ENT-4"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The true edge of an executed lower <= upper guard contributes one ordinary difference bound. The deterministic affine discharge route consumes that same relation directly, admits upper - lower without an explicit invariant certificate, and the closed call observes 9 - 4 as 5."}
{"id": "op2-neg-div-wrap", "rules": ["OP-2", "OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Division and remainder carry no wrap mode [OP-2]; idiv.wrap names no table op and must be rejected."}
{"id": "op3-pos-fadd-strict", "rules": ["OP-3"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A float op that ROUNDS carries .strict [OP-3]: fadd.strict is IEEE-754, no reassociation, no contraction \u2014 the family a future fast-math mode would relax."}
{"id": "op3-neg-exact-dotted", "rules": ["OP-3", "OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Float ops that are exact or exact-selection are dotless [OP-3]: fneg carries no .strict. OP-3 fixes the dotted/dotless convention but states no rejection of its own; `fneg.strict` is an OPNAME absent from the operation-family inventory, which OP-1 makes a hard error."}
{"id": "op4-neg-index-undischarged", "rules": ["OP-4", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An unproven parameter offset is a compile-time rejection under discharge-or-reject [OP-4]. The diagnostic carries the ENT-6 residual `i < len(b)` and points to a dominating branch, function requirement, loop invariant, or checked source proof as the repair."}
{"id": "op4-pos-index-discharged", "rules": ["OP-4", "ENT-3"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The deterministic unsigned-remainder range establishes the exact upper bound needed by the following fixed-array subscript [ENT-3, OP-4]."}
{"id": "op6-pos-cvt-total", "rules": ["OP-6"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "cvt on a value-preserving (total) pair has signature (Src)->own Dst with no Result [OP-6]: i8->i32 is one of the 29 total pairs."}
{"id": "op6-pos-cvt-checked", "rules": ["OP-6"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "cvt on a non-total pair returns Result<Dst, NarrowError> and is EXACT [OP-6]: i32->i8 yields Err(NarrowError()) when not exactly representable; it never rounds, truncates, or saturates."}
{"id": "op6-neg-cvt-identity", "rules": ["OP-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-6"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "cvt<T, T> is not an operation [OP-6]; a same-type conversion must be rejected."}
{"id": "op7-pos-name-convention", "rules": ["OP-7"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Operation names follow the convention [OP-7]: a truthful domain prefix (i/f/b/e) plus a .mode suffix iff on a mode axis. This existing case exercises iadd.checked and dotless ieq; it does not itself exercise eeq."}
{"id": "op7-neg-missing-prefix", "rules": ["OP-7", "OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An arithmetic/logic/bit/compare op carries a domain prefix [OP-7]; unprefixed add.wrap names no table op and must be rejected."}
{"id": "op8-pos-iand", "rules": ["OP-8"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "iand/ior/ixor lower to LLVM and/or/xor and are total [OP-8]; the bitwise ops carry no mode suffix."}
{"id": "op8-neg-rotate-checked", "rules": ["OP-8", "OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "irotl/irotr take the amount modulo width and are dotless-total [OP-8]; `irotl.checked` is a well-formed operation token but names no table row and is rejected under OP-1."}
{"id": "op8-pos-u64-shift-u32", "rules": ["OP-1", "OP-8"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Shift amounts are uniformly u32 even when the shifted value is u64; 16 >> 2 equals 4 and runs."}
{"id": "op8-pos-integer-family", "rules": ["OP-1", "OP-2", "OP-8"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A checksum-style scalar mix combines rotate, xor, wrapping addition, population count, and unsigned minimum through the general integer path; it runs without project- or function-specific handling."}
{"id": "op9-pos-buffer-new", "rules": ["OP-9", "FN-8", "ENT-6", "STOR-6"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "buffer_new::<i32>(n, value) accepts a runtime length after the source contract establishes both a small numeric upper bound and `buffer_fits::<i32>(n)` [FN-8, OP-9, ENT-6]. Target qualification then proves that the entire reachable byte range fits the selected allocation and address domains [STOR-6]."}
{"id": "fn1-pos-signature-driven-call", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A fn's signature states param modes/types and return mode/type; main calls it relying only on that signature (FN-1). Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "fn3-pos-contract-conform", "rules": ["FN-3"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A contract declares fn signatures and a conform binds a member fn for a type (FN-3): the contract/conform mechanism."}
{"id": "fn3-neg-two-conformances", "rules": ["FN-3"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Two conform blocks for the same (type, contract) pair violate FN-3's at-most-one-conformance rule."}
{"id": "fn4-pos-law-in-contract", "rules": ["FN-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A contract states a semantic law name from the closed {associative, commutative, identity} table (FN-4): 'law associative(combine);'."}
{"id": "fn4-neg-bad-lawname", "rules": ["FN-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The semantic law name distributive is outside FN-4's closed law-name table and is rejected by FN-4."}
{"id": "fn5-pos-match-dispatch", "rules": ["FN-5"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Behavior is selected by match over a closed enum (FN-5's 'closed-set dispatch is match'), not a function value; there is no fn-value construct to violate. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "fn6-pos-recursion", "rules": ["FN-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A self-recursive fn (sum 0..n) is permitted (FN-6); sumto(4)==10. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "fn6-neg-polymorphic-recursion", "rules": ["FN-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-6"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A generic fn whose recursive call instantiates the callee at a fixed type (i32) instead of the caller's own type parameter T is polymorphic recursion, rejected by FN-6's syntactic rule."}
{"id": "fn7-pos-single-main", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Exactly one command fn main returns a named own ExitStatus, has no contract, and is not source-callable [FN-7]. A command entry may select zero standard capabilities. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "fn7-neg-two-mains", "rules": ["TYPE-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-6"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The later of two fn main declarations is the TYPE-6 duplicate declaration selected by the active specification's exact diagnostic order."}
# obligation discharge: source facts and the entailment fragment (section 18)
{"id": "ent1-pos-instantiation-judged-at-value", "rules": ["ENT-1", "FN-2", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The generic source-schema judgment receives an executed length guard for its subscript; the n = 4 concrete instance is then independently rechecked and runs with the expected value [ENT-1, FN-2, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent1-neg-instantiation-judged-at-value", "rules": ["ENT-1", "FN-2", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An unguarded subscript in a const-generic source body is rejected by its source-schema ordinary judgment; a later concrete call cannot retroactively admit the body [ENT-1, FN-2, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent2-pos-array-length-survives-root-replace", "rules": ["ENT-2", "ENT-4", "ENT-5", "SET-2", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "[ENT-2] the implicit `len(P) = N` of an `array<T, N>` place holds at every program point. An element read feeding a branch condition puts a join on the path, and the SET-2 replace of the array's root binding then kills every relation supported by that place [ENT-5]. The length equality is a function of the place's type rather than a fact that had to survive, so closure still discharges the later subscript [ENT-4, OP-4]; the program observes the exchanged values and exits 0."}
{"id": "ent2-neg-no-fact-across-call", "rules": ["ENT-2", "OP-4", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "No fact crosses a call boundary [ENT-2]. A checked comparison bounds the argument, but the value an ordinary call returns is a fresh term carrying no relation to it, so the subscript is undischarged and the residual names the returned binding."}
{"id": "ent2-neg-expired-spelling-inherits-nothing", "rules": ["ENT-2", "ENT-5", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Facts attach to terms, not spellings. An arm-local checked-branch bound dies when its scope ends, and a later binding reusing that spelling inherits nothing [ENT-2, ENT-5, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent3-pos-s1-branch-fact", "rules": ["ENT-3", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Source S1: a match whose scrutinee has comparison origin establishes that relation at the True() arm's entry and its exact negation at the False() arm's entry [ENT-3]; the guarded arm discharges the subscript with no runtime bounds branch."}
{"id": "ent3-pos-s4-requires-fact", "rules": ["ENT-3", "ENT-2", "FN-8", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Source S4: each complete static requires-clause goal, with contract definitions expanded and clause locals substituted out, is admitted independently at body entry [ENT-2]. Every ordinary caller proves that goal before the call [FN-8]."}
{"id": "ent3-pos-s5-set-commit-image", "rules": ["ENT-3", "ENT-5", "SET-1", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Source S5 publishes a direct-place set commit's literal, term-copy, or total-conversion equality after the old target facts are killed. Here start = back carries the already proved n - 8 bound to the subscript without a runtime check."}
{"id": "ent3-pos-s6-allocation-length-fact", "rules": ["ENT-3", "OP-9", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Source S6: buffer_new establishes len(b) = n on the normal continuation [ENT-3, OP-9] and the S5 copy equality carries the literal into n, so a constant offset below that length discharges. The length is a declared allocation property, not a writer statement."}
{"id": "ent3-pos-bor-guard-decomposition", "rules": ["ENT-3", "OP-4", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ENT-3 signed Boolean decomposition [O11]: establishing a signed goal also establishes every member of its structural decomposition, and decomp of a negative bor(A, B) is the conjunctive read {-A, -B}. The else edge of a two-sided bor guard therefore establishes -below and -above, whose projections negate to symbol >= 0 and symbol < 4; with len(table) = 4 the guarded subscript discharges and the program runs. This case replaces ent3-neg-bor-no-comparison-origin, whose subject was that the same guard establishes nothing."}
{"id": "ent3-pos-band-check-decomposition", "rules": ["ENT-3", "OP-4", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "On the true edge of band(A, B), ENT-3 decomposes the condition into +A and +B. The two projected upper bounds independently discharge the two fixed-array subscript obligations."}
{"id": "ent4-pos-transitivity-discharges", "rules": ["ENT-4", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Ordinary executed control establishes i at most j and j below room; closure transitivity derives i below room and discharges the subscript [ENT-4, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent4-pos-disequality-strengthens", "rules": ["ENT-4", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Ordinary executed control establishes i at most room and i unequal to room; closure strengthens the relation to the strict bound required by the subscript [ENT-4, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent4-neg-nonstrict-bound-underivable", "rules": ["ENT-4", "OP-4", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Derivability is exact: a - b <= c is derivable only when the closed state contains a - b <= c' with c' <= c [ENT-4]. A checked i <= room does not derive the obligation's i - room <= -1, correctly, since i may equal the length."}
{"id": "ent4-pos-contradictory-state-discharges", "rules": ["ENT-3", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Ordinary executed control establishes the real subscript bound. A written proof conclusion has authority only after its premises are checked, so it cannot manufacture an unreachable fact or use explosion to admit a partial operation [ENT-3, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent5-pos-element-write-preserves-length", "rules": ["ENT-5", "SET-1", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The support of a length term is the root binding of the place, not its element storage, so an element write never kills a length fact [ENT-5]: a buffer's length is fixed at allocation. The later subscript still discharges."}
{"id": "ent5-pos-join-keeps-common-bound", "rules": ["ENT-5", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The join keeps, for each ordered term pair, the weakest bound held by all joined states [ENT-5]. Both arms establish the same strict bound, so the continuation keeps it and the subscript after the match discharges."}
{"id": "ent5-pos-close-before-scope-kill", "rules": ["ENT-4", "ENT-5", "ENT-3", "SET-1", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The true arm derives out = next = i + 1 and i < 3. ENT-4 closure materializes out < 4 before next leaves scope; ENT-5 then kills every conclusion still naming next, while the survivor-only bound discharges the continuation subscript."}
{"id": "ent5-pos-project-middle-before-set", "rules": ["ENT-3", "ENT-4", "ENT-5", "SET-1", "OP-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The entry facts x <= middle <= 254 imply the survivor-only bound x <= 254. Before set replaces the middle vertex, complete pre-kill closure materializes that consequence, so the following exact x + 1 is admitted. ent5-neg-kill-on-write separately pins that replacing a conclusion endpoint still removes its old bound."}
{"id": "ent5-pos-close-before-set-strengthening", "rules": ["ENT-4", "ENT-5", "SET-1", "OP-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The entry facts value <= byte_limit and value != byte_limit first strengthen to value < byte_limit; the implicit u8 ceiling then yields value <= 254. Complete ENT-4 closure materializes that survivor-only consequence before set replaces byte_limit, so the following exact increment remains proved."}
{"id": "ent5-neg-join-takes-weakest-bound", "rules": ["ENT-5", "OP-4", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The join keeps the weakest (largest-constant) bound held by all joined states [ENT-5]. One arm establishes i - room <= -1 and the other only i - room <= 0, so the continuation carries the non-strict bound and the subscript after the match is undischarged."}
{"id": "ent5-neg-kill-on-write", "rules": ["ENT-5", "ENT-3", "SET-1", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A checked branch bounds offset; a later set kills that old fact before S5 establishes only offset = start. Because start itself is unbounded, the new post-write equality cannot resurrect offset < len(b), and OP-4 still rejects."}
{"id": "ent5-neg-loop-rule-drops-preloop-fact", "rules": ["ENT-5", "OP-4", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An else-free false edge carries the offset write to the next loop head, so the continuing kill removes the dominating checked-branch bound and the subscript remains undischarged [ENT-5, ENT-6, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent5-pos-return-does-not-kill-loop-head-fact", "rules": ["ENT-5", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A return edge cannot reach a later iteration head, so its scope-exit kills do not remove a pre-loop bound there [ENT-5]. Else-free false edges may continue, but neither kills the bound, so the head subscript discharges [OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent5-neg-callee-uniq-buffer-replace-kills-length", "rules": ["ENT-5", "EFF-2", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-4"}, "status": "xfail", "reason": "The current compiler derives the element flag of a projected callee write from the actual's syntactic shape, so every callee write through a &uniq buffer<T> actual is classified as an element write and no length fact ever dies at such a call. The stale length discharges the subscript and the program is wrongly accepted.", "doc": "The callee replaces the whole referent of its &uniq buffer parameter, and [EFF-2] projects that writes occurrence onto the actual place, which is the support member of the caller's length term. [ENT-5] clause (a) makes a whole-place replace of a buffer kill that buffer's length facts, so the allocation fact len(line) = 10 must not survive the call and the later subscript of offset 9 into what is now a two-byte allocation must be rejected at [OP-4]. The current compiler accepts it instead, because clause (b)'s element-write classification is taken from the actual's shape rather than from what the callee does; the accepted program performs an out-of-bounds heap read with no claim and no runtime check. This case tracks that gap until the classification fix or the container and ownership redesign lands, and turns into an XPASS the moment either does."}
{"id": "ent6-neg-join-one-arm-advances-accumulator", "rules": ["INV-1", "ENT-5", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "INV-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "One arm of the body join advances the accumulator and the other leaves it, so the incoming value images sum + wide and sum share no nonconstant coefficient vector and differ by more than a constant. [ENT-6] gives the binding one fresh full-type atom at the join and [ENT-5]'s all-predecessor intersection keeps no bound that only the advancing arm established, so [INV-1] cannot re-establish the header target over that atom. The rejection is mandatory even though the relation holds on every execution; the join is deliberately not path-sensitive."}
{"id": "ent6-pos-join-value-if-lifted-addend", "rules": ["ENT-6", "INV-1", "GIVE-1", "OP-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The same fold with the choice lifted out of the control join and into the addend. The value_if delivers one owned value [GIVE-1], the body applies one unconditional update, and the single value image sum + addend leaves the [ENT-6] join nothing to weaken. A local invariant bounds the delivered addend by the byte ceiling, so [INV-1] re-establishes the header target and [OP-2] admits the exact addition; the explicit result control observes the even-index total 6."}
