{"id":"stor5-neg-arena-new-region-bearing","rules":["STOR-2","STOR-5","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"arena_new cannot hide a direct slice in arena content; STOR-5 owns the region-bearing content rejection before unsupported arena execution or lowering matters."}
{"id":"own5-neg-slice-value-match","rules":["OWN-5","GIVE-1","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A slice-valued let-initializer match is rejected at the complete value_match because v0.17 defines direct-return choice but no branch-dependent slice ownership join."}
{"id":"stor5-pos-direct-nested-slice-type","rules":["STOR-5","FN-2","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"pending","reason":"The compiler intentionally leaves non-flat slice elements unsupported; v0.17 preserves this direct type-formation case rather than misreporting it as an FN-2 or STOR-5 source rejection.","doc":"A direct slice whose element type is another direct slice remains type-formable, and len may name that operation element type; no stored-content or nominal/function generic-argument position is introduced."}
{"id": "accept-sysentry-command-all-inputs", "rules": ["FN-7", "SYS-1", "SYS-2", "SYS-3", "SYS-5", "SYS-10", "SYS-12", "SYS-13", "EFF-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A command entry selecting all five standard input rows in strictly increasing table-ordinal order is admitted [FN-7]; command.stdout and command.stderr are distinct inputs sharing one type [SYS-12], command.files supplies the ordinary FileFactory [SYS-10], and the entry's exact row is writes(cwd), contributed by DirectoryRead release while FileFactory releases by logical consume [SYS-5, EFF-2]."}
{"id": "accept-sysentry-command-no-inputs", "rules": ["FN-7", "SYS-3", "SYS-13"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A command entry that selects no standard input row is admitted and receives none [FN-7]. The complete SYS-3 declaration domain is present in every unit independently of the entry's selected inputs; this entry simply requests zero standard capabilities. exit_status is total and pure with no failure outcome [SYS-13]."}
{"id": "fn7-neg-missing-command-marker", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A main declaration with a named unit result but no `command` marker is not an alternate entry form. FN-7 rejects the missing marker, so every accepted program enters only through the uncallable command boundary."}
{"id": "reject-type5-system-arg-mismatch", "rules": ["TYPE-5"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A helper accepts the admitted opaque Args type, while its command caller passes unit. Exact argument conformance rejects the value-type mismatch under TYPE-5."}
{"id": "accept-sysname-near-lookalike", "rules": ["SYS-1", "SYS-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The system declaration domain reserves exact operation spellings, while an ordinary near-lookalike remains a source function and runs normally [SYS-1, SYS-3]."}
{"id": "reject-sysname-collision-in-kind-unit", "rules": ["SYS-1", "SYS-3", "TYPE-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-6"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The complete system declaration domain is present in every unit [SYS-3], so a source declaration equal to an inventory entry's spelling in the same domain is a collision, the unit is rejected, and neither declaration resolves [SYS-1]. No proximity, declaration order, scope depth, or expected type decides it. This is declaration-inventory rank 5, which cites TYPE-6 and reports only the system conflict."}
{"id": "reject-sysexit-return-u8-no-conversion", "rules": ["FN-7", "SYS-13", "FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ExitStatus is opaque with exactly one constructor and no implicit conversion from u8 [SYS-13, TYPE-4], so without the stated constructor a command entry's return value is unwritable. A return whose produced type differs from the enclosing function's written rtype is a hard error citing FN-1 at the return_stmt node, not a system-domain rule."}
{"id": "reject-form1-service-leading-construct", "rules": ["FORM-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The v0.33 entry grammar admits only the fixed `command` marker. A leading `service` spelling is an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1 before entry semantics."}
{"id": "reject-form1-embedded-leading-construct", "rules": ["FORM-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The v0.33 entry grammar admits only the fixed `command` marker. A leading `embedded` spelling is an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1 before entry semantics."}
{"id": "reject-form1-daemon-leading-construct", "rules": ["FORM-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The v0.33 grammar no longer admits an arbitrary IDENT as a program kind. A leading `daemon` spelling is therefore an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-label-out-of-order", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Selected standard inputs appear in strictly increasing table-ordinal order, so declared order is the one legal byte sequence [FORM-1, GRAM-8]. `command.cwd` (ordinal 1) written before `command.args` (ordinal 0) is out of order and is a hard error citing FN-7 at that input_label node."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-label-repeated", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Every standard input row is optional and each may be selected at most once; a repeated `command.args` label is a hard error citing FN-7 at that input_label node. Distinct binder identifiers do not make it two inputs, because ordinal identity, never binder or type identity, selects the supplied value."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-label-unknown", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The standard input table for kind `command` is closed at five rows, so `command.env` selects none and is a hard error citing FN-7 at that input_label node. A later kind table adds rows by specification amendment, never by a writer naming one."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-label-outside-entry", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "No declaration other than the sole command entry carries an input_label child, so an input_label on a parameter of any other fn_decl is a hard error citing FN-7 at that input_label node. This keeps each standard input an explicitly selected owned value rather than ambient state any function can request."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-input-type-mismatch", "rules": ["FN-7", "TYPE-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A selected parameter's written mode and type equal its table row exactly, with no conversion, default, or inferred mode [TYPE-4, TYPE-5]. `command.args` written as `own DirectoryRead` is a hard error citing FN-7 at that complete param node, and the label rather than the written type is what selects the row."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-call-to-kind-entry", "rules": ["FN-7", "PROG-3"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Main is the sole command entry and program start invokes it once [PROG-3]. It is never a source-callable function, so a source call resolving to main is rejected under FN-7."}
{"id": "accept-sysrelease-return-unit-declared", "rules": ["EFF-1", "EFF-2", "STOR-3", "SYS-4", "SYS-5", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The EFF-2 canonical case named by the specification: a nongeneric function whose only parameter is own ReadFile and whose body is exactly return unit exhibits writes(file). That exact state write is the compiler-derived close attempt on the return edge [STOR-3, SYS-5, SYS-11]; release-complete means no source terminal operation is needed."}
{"id": "reject-syseff-return-unit-pure", "rules": ["EFF-1", "EFF-2", "STOR-3", "SYS-5", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "EFF-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The omission direction of the canonical release case: although the written body has no syntactic action, releasing the owned ReadFile contributes writes(file). Declaring the empty row pure therefore produces an undeclared state write and is rejected at the function's effects node [EFF-1, EFF-2]."}
{"id": "reject-syseff-declared-unexhibited", "rules": ["EFF-2", "SYS-5", "SYS-9"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "EFF-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "args_count reads the explicit Args state, so declaring pure omits reads(args). Args release is a logical consume with no additional state effect [SYS-5, SYS-9]; EFF-2 rejects the exact undeclared read rather than inferring it from the borrow lifetime."}
{"id": "accept-syseff-conditional-release-union", "rules": ["EFF-2", "SYS-5", "SYS-6", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The release contribution is the union of every release action that may run on any edge of the conservative structural normal-control graph, so an owner released on only one match arm still contributes its row. Only the Ok arm ever holds a ReadFile; IoError takes no release row at all [SYS-5], and open_read's outcome type is the Result instantiation that makes the two arms the complete edge set [SYS-6]."}
{"id": "reject-syseff-conditional-release-narrow", "rules": ["EFF-2", "SYS-5", "SYS-6", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "EFF-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The near-miss a writer argues is correct by reading `conditional` as `optional`: running on only some paths never weakens the release contribution, and no path condition, constant evaluation, discharged law, optimizer fact, or backend reachability judgment removes an edge from that graph. Omitting the row is an undeclared-but-exhibited rejection citing EFF-2."}
{"id": "accept-syseff-pure-immutable-only", "rules": ["EFF-1", "EFF-2", "SYS-4", "SYS-9"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An immutable Args snapshot admits concurrent reads, but it remains an explicit input value. args_count therefore contributes reads(args), while the borrow region separately governs storage lifetime."}
{"id": "reject-syseff-pure-member-binds-release", "rules": ["FN-3", "EFF-2", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A fn_sig has no body and no compiler-derived release, so the member may declare pure without its own EFF-2 body judgment. The bound function independently exhibits writes(file) through ReadFile release; signature equality compares formal state paths, so binding it to the pure member is an FN-3 mismatch."}
{"rule": "PATH-1", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "Relative-path construction is a runtime Result outcome, not a compile-time verdict: the dossier phrases that read like rejection (NUL, target-root prefix) are PathInvalid values the program matches. The catalog's group C cases state the behavior, and every one of them needs the fixture and argv arrangement this task's schema extension adds; they land with the runtime lane in task 0014. The no-allocation, no-copy, same-lease retype half is a codegen inspection with no source-to-verdict pair."}
{"rule":"PATH-2","covered_by":"task-0014-runtime-lane","reason":"Directory-relative resolution is process-equivalent by design and provides no confinement guarantee, so the observable content is a runtime open against a real directory fixture rather than an acceptance judgment. A target with no directory-relative facility fails qualification instead of emulating the operation by prefix concatenation [QUAL-1]."}
{"rule": "HOST-1", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "Code-unit width and family are properties of the selected target, not a portable source layout: no source construct observes them and no operation yields them as source values of a fixed width. The lossless-preservation guarantee is witnessed by the non-UTF-8 argv round-trip case, which needs the argv byte list this task adds and executes in task 0014; a target outside both families fails qualification instead."}
{"rule": "HOST-2", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "The two conversion routes differ only in what they validate and what they report, which is a runtime outcome comparison (Utf8Invalid, Utf8CopyInvalid, CopyTooSmall, and the sentinel-buffer no-write checks) rather than an acceptance judgment. The no-implicit-conversion half is TYPE-4's general rule, already exercised by the existing conversion-rejection cases and by reject-sysexit-return-u8-no-conversion."}
{"rule": "HOST-3", "covered_by": "compiler-checked-program", "reason": "The specification states outright that retention of lease identity in the checked program serves auditing and lowering, is not a source-acceptance judgment, and refuses no program. A lease is neither a borrow nor a region-bearing type, so STOR-5 places no restriction on storing one and OWN-5 provenance does not describe it: there is no source-to-verdict pair to write. The command-lifetime backing premise is a target guarantee enforced at qualification [QUAL-2]."}
{"rule": "SYS-7", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "The twenty-eight portable classes are the failure vocabulary a running program branches on; the class-to-verdict content is the group D open-error cases, which need real filesystem fixtures. Exhaustive branching on the class is ERR-2's general judgment, already covered by the existing exhaustive-match cases, and a corpus case cannot observe a target's native-to-portable mapping or its use of Other and Unsupported."}
{"rule":"SYS-8","covered_by":"task-0014-runtime-lane","reason":"One-attempt transfer semantics are observable only by executing a transfer: a short success is not EOF, ReadBytes(next) is reported only for next > start, and the returned absolute endpoint becomes the next cursor. Invalid ranges reject statically before host access and create no runtime proof fallback. File fixtures and redirection cover the runtime outcomes; signatures and call-scoped borrow modes are SYS-2 inventory data."}
{"rule": "EFF-5", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "Ordinary owned places, exact effect paths, and loans determine coexistence. The runnable redirected-output case uses two successive unique loans of one Output and observes their byte order; distinct Output values remain independent even if environment redirection aliases their physical sink. Cross-call overlap is a compiler permission and completion-lowering property rather than a second source verdict."}
{"rule": "QUAL-1", "covered_by": "compiler-target-qualification", "reason": "A target-qualification failure is explicitly not a source-language rejection and cites no language rule, so it can never be a reject verdict; this is the stop the new `unsupported` expectation kind exists for, and it becomes expressible once a case can pin an unqualified target. The qualification table is compiler-internal data and the language defines no registry, negotiation protocol, dynamic loading, or plugin interface, so no source construct reaches it."}
{"rule": "QUAL-2", "covered_by": "compiler-target-qualification", "reason": "All four stated guarantees are target properties with nothing in a program to prove: command-lifetime argument backing, a lossless code-unit family, directory-relative resolution, and directory enumeration. Qualification establishes each required guarantee before entry; absence stops compilation or startup rather than entering with a weaker substitute or returning a source outcome."}
{"rule": "QUAL-3", "covered_by": "compiler-emitted-code-inspection", "reason": "The required emitted shape — static selection with no runtime operation-ID switch or handle lookup, at most one direct host call, no heap allocation, no data copy, no global lock, no per-call signal-disposition operation, and an inlined or immaterial wrapper — is established by inspection of emitted code and symbols, which the specification states is not a machine-checked language judgment. The one source-visible consequence, a closed output destination arriving as a recoverable outcome rather than a signal death, is the catalog's broken-pipe case."}
{"rule": "GATE-2", "covered_by": "gated-family", "reason": "A separation rule between the system domain and the boundary-construct family, in the same class as the GATE-1 and LEDGER-1 policy annotations. Its content is that a program calling system operations contains no gated construct and remains a kernel program, and conversely that general FFI and writer-declared external signatures stay reserved to that family. Neither direction has a writer-emittable construct to accept or violate: adding a system operation is a specification amendment, never a gate approval or a target-implementation act."}
{"rule": "PAR-1", "covered_by": "compiler-permission-judgment", "reason": "A permission rule grants an implementation room to overlap sibling calls without changing source acceptance. Compiler actualization tests run the same emitted program at multiple worker counts and against sequential lowering, checking identical published bytes and typed call outcomes; conformance cases cover the ownership, loans, effects, and exact places from which the permission is derived. A corpus verdict cannot distinguish whether a valid permission was taken."}
{"rule": "PAR-2", "covered_by": "compiler-permission-judgment", "reason": "The counted-loop half of the same permission, and covered for the same reason: it grants an implementation the room to overlap the iterations of a counted for and to choose the combination tree over the enumerated exactly-associative operations, and forbids nothing. No program's acceptance moves and no verdict moves. The one observable consequence — that a regrouped fold publishes the bytes the sequential fold publishes — is established by the compiler's own cases, which run one emitted module at ten worker settings, against the lowering that splits nothing, and against a two-sidedness table over every admitted combine at every width it carries."}
{"rule": "PAR-3", "covered_by": "compiler-permission-judgment", "reason": "The staged half of the same permission family, and covered for the same reason as PAR-1 and PAR-2: it grants an implementation the room to overlap the remainder of one iteration against the prologue of a later one and forbids nothing a writer can write. No program's acceptance moves and no verdict moves, so the seven accept-par3-* cases can only establish the direction a corpus can see \u2014 that each of these shapes, granted and denied alike, compiles exactly as it did before the rule existed. What each shape's staged verdict actually is, and which numbered condition and which place produced it, is established by the compiler's own judgment and ledger cases, which assert the disposition of every place in both directions and pin that a degraded entailment state produces the identical table. The denied cases are maintained because the rule's whole risk is a loop that should have been refused and was not: the loop-carried scratch buffer, the loop-carried marker byte a whole-place write-before-read rule would admit, the read that precedes every write of its storage, the enumeration cursor no replication can ever reach, and the early return taken after later iterations have already submitted opens."}
{"id": "run-sysexit-code-0", "rules": ["SYS-13", "FN-7", "PROG-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-13: the target maps the returned command code exactly onto the host process status, and exit_status is total with no failure outcome. Zero is the first of the three codes the first slice's command programs distinguish; a conforming implementation supplies the declared standard inputs and invokes the entry once [PROG-3]."}
{"id": "run-sysexit-code-1", "rules": ["SYS-13", "FN-7", "PROG-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 1}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-13: the same mapping at a nonzero code. Kept as its own case because a target that collapsed every nonzero status onto one value would still pass the zero case, and the closed 0..255 range is the whole promise."}
{"id": "run-sysexit-code-2", "rules": ["SYS-13", "FN-7", "PROG-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 2}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-13: the third distinguished code, which together with the other two pins the mapping as the identity rather than a success or failure collapse."}
{"id": "run-sysarg-count-and-get", "rules": ["SYS-9", "SYS-6", "HOST-3", "QUAL-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "616c706861", "62657461"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-9: args_count is total and arg_get returns Ok at a present position and InvalidIndex beyond the last, returning no value. The arranged vector is the complete native one, so position 0 is the invoked name and the count is three; each returned HostString is an inline lease over the command-lifetime backing QUAL-2 requires, released by logical consume [SYS-5]."}
{"id": "run-syshost-nontext-argv-bytes-roundtrip", "rules": ["HOST-1", "HOST-2", "SYS-8", "SYS-9"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "61ff62"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Every argument retains its complete native code-unit sequence [HOST-1]. The start-zero lossless copy returns absolute endpoint 3, preserves the lone 0xff unchanged, and leaves the byte beyond `[start,next)` untouched [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-syshost-nontext-argv-utf8-invalid", "rules": ["HOST-2", "SYS-6", "SYS-9"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "61ff62"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "HOST-2: the text route on the same argument the lossless route copies verbatim. It validates the complete code-unit sequence and reports an explicit invalid-text outcome, never a replacement code point, a dropped code unit, or a truncated encoding. The two routes are the only way a host string reaches source content, and each names the route it takes."}
{"id": "run-syshost-copybytes-toosmall-unchanged", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-6", "HOST-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "616263646566"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-8: on every recoverable failure of a copy operation the whole buffer is unchanged, and CopyTooSmall(required) states the exact length the destination range must have for the same call to succeed. The program keeps running, so a sentinel prefill directly witnesses the no-write guarantee; invalid ranges are separate static rejections and never reach this outcome."}
{"id": "run-syshost-copyutf8-invalid-unchanged", "rules": ["SYS-8", "HOST-2", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "61ff62"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-8: host_copy_utf8 validates and measures the encoding first and returns Utf8CopyInvalid without writing any byte. The destination is large enough here, so only validation can have refused the transfer, and the surviving sentinel shows no partial encoding was written."}
{"id": "run-syshost-copyutf8-toosmall-unchanged", "rules": ["SYS-8", "HOST-2", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "616263646566"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-8: the other refusal of the text route, kept separate because one is a validation failure and the other a capacity failure. Valid text too long for the destination reports the exact encoded length through Utf8CopyTooSmall and writes nothing."}
{"id":"reject-syshost-copybytes-start-after-end","rules":["SYS-8","ERR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SYS-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Start greater than end refutes the first SYS-8 range goal statically. The rejected operation has no host call, source read, destination write, or runtime fallback."}
{"id": "reject-syshost-copybytes-start-beyond-buffer", "rules": ["SYS-8", "ERR-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SYS-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An empty range is legal only when its endpoint lies inside the destination. Start equals end at 8 over a four-byte buffer, so the second SYS-8 goal is refuted statically."}
{"id":"reject-syshost-copybytes-end-beyond-buffer","rules":["SYS-8","ERR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SYS-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Start lies inside the destination but end exceeds its length. SYS-8 rejects the call statically instead of attaching an operation-internal runtime check."}
{"id": "run-syspath-relative-basic", "rules": ["PATH-1", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "666978747572652e747874"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "PATH-1: construction from one host string succeeds when the complete code-unit sequence contains no NUL code unit and begins with no target-root prefix. It consumes its input on success and on failure, and success retypes the same inline lease with no allocation and no code-unit change; the no-allocation half is inspection-only, and the Ok outcome is the testable half."}
{"id":"run-syspath-dotdot-preserved","rules":["PATH-1","PATH-2"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"arrange":{"argv":["63617365","2e2f696e6e65722f2e2e2f696e6e65722f666978747572652e747874"]},"status":"runnable","doc":"PATH-1 preserves every admitted code unit exactly, including . and .. components and every separator, and performs no normalization, canonicalization, prefix stripping, or component collapse. The first slice's DirectoryRead state is process-equivalent and provides no confinement guarantee [PATH-2]."}
{"id": "run-syspath-absolute-rejected", "rules": ["PATH-1", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "2f616273656e74"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "PATH-1: a Unix-family leading separator is a member of that target's target-root prefix set, so construction fails and yields no path value. This is a runtime PathInvalid outcome the program matches, not a compile-time rejection; PathError is an ordinary outcome type and never substitutes for IoError's InvalidPath [SYS-6]."}
{"id": "run-sysdir-open-notfound", "rules": ["SYS-7", "SYS-10", "PATH-2", "ERR-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "616273656e742e747874"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-7: a target maps every native failure it can produce onto exactly one portable class, and the class is the sole portable discriminator that exhaustive control flow branches on [ERR-2]. Opening a name no object in the initial directory carries is NotFound. The case reads no detail field, because code and origin are diagnostic data and no portable semantics is defined in terms of them."}
{"id": "run-sysfile-empty", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-11", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "656d7074792e747874"], "files": [{"path": "656d7074792e747874", "bytes": ""}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "On a zero-byte file the first read_at over a nonempty destination range is ReadEnd, never ReadBytes, because the latter carries a success endpoint strictly beyond start [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-sysfile-short", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "73686f72742e747874"], "files": [{"path": "73686f72742e747874", "bytes": "616263"}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A short success is not end of input. ReadBytes(next) names the absolute endpoint of the changed prefix `[start,next)`; only the following call that observes no byte reports ReadEnd [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-sysfile-exact", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "65786163742e747874"], "files": [{"path": "65786163742e747874", "bytes": "6162636465666768"}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A file exactly filling `[start,end)` returns ReadBytes(next: end), not ReadEnd. A further call is required before end of input can be reported [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-sysfile-multichunk", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "706167652e747874"], "files": [{"path": "706167652e747874", "bytes": "303132333435363738396162636465666768696a"}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Repeated reads advance the explicit file offset in source while each ReadBytes payload is the absolute endpoint of that call's changed destination prefix. The final short read changes only `[start,next)` and a later positioned call reports ReadEnd [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-sysout-basic-write", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-12"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "One write_once makes at most one host output attempt. Its Ok payload is the absolute accepted endpoint, and the accepted source prefix is `[start,next)` [SYS-8, SYS-12]."}
{"id": "run-sysout-redirect-same-sink-order", "rules": ["EFF-5", "SYS-12", "SYS-8", "PATH-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "636f6d62696e6564"], "redirect": {"stdout": "combined", "stderr": "combined"}}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Two calls take successive unique loans of one Output, so the second cannot start until completion returns the first loan [EFF-5, SYS-12]. Reading the redirected sink back with read_at witnesses the resulting bytes [SYS-8, PATH-2]; no order is inferred between distinct Output values merely because the environment aliases their physical sink."}
{"id": "form3-pos-index-ordinary-ident", "rules": ["FORM-3", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "v0.22 releases index from the fixed grammar atoms: an ordinary IDENT binding named index is declared and used as a subscript offset, pinning the released spelling [FORM-3, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent3-pos-s11-counted-range-run", "rules": ["GRAM-4", "GRAM-6", "FN-1", "ENT-3", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A normal counted form executes 0..4 as an ascending half-open range, executes 4..4 zero times, and honors a break naming the counted loop after one local iteration [FN-1, GRAM-4, GRAM-6]. On both entered bodies, S11 proves the binder below the captured upper constant and thereby discharges each constant-array subscript before lowering [ENT-3, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent2-neg-counted-subscript-endpoint", "rules": ["ENT-2", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "ENT-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Each endpoint is a grammar-valid atom and each constant array subscript has its own discharged bounds obligation [OP-4], but ENT-2 admits only a preceding term or constant as a counted endpoint. A subscripted place remains excluded and is rejected at the first complete endpoint atom with the prescribed let-binding restructuring."}
{"id": "set1-neg-counted-binder-write", "rules": ["SET-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SET-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The counted binder is immutable compiler-updated own u64 state. A source set targeting it is rejected under SET-1 at the complete binder place; the hidden normal-fallthrough increment grants no source writability."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-plain-direct-result","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A verified plain-result postcondition is published at a fresh direct ordinary let, and the earlier callee summary discharges the relay's selected return before ordinary result control observes the value."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-ok-selected-receiver","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-5","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A verified Ok payload relation is transferred by a direct call match and its immediate bare payload-to-outer write; the caller's selected return and ordinary result control observe the replaced value."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-no-selected-normal-exit","rules":["FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An Ok selector with only an Err normal return has no selected normal exit; FN-9 rejects at the selector with the residual no selected normal exit."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-unproved-selected-return","rules":["FN-9","ENT-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The first source-ordered selected return does not establish the declared output equality, so FN-9 rejects that unproved return even though the later return is discharged."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-entry-image-kill","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An overlapping write permanently invalidates the formal's entry image before the selected return; FN-9 rejects the resulting unproved output relation."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-same-scc-summary","rules":["FN-9","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A seedless mutually recursive component cannot use a same-SCC postcondition summary to prove either returned call result; an independently proved recursive control in the same unit shows recursion alone is not the rejection."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-stage8b-bit-sources","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Unsigned direct-let iand establishes an independent result bound for each admitted operand, while direct wrapping shifts led by a typed literal one and an earlier named-constant one establish nonzero results; exact requirements consume all four S7 sources."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-stage8b-local-one","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A local binding whose value equals one is not the direct typed-literal or earlier named-constant identity required by the shift source; the exact nonzero call requirement remains unproved and FN-8 rejects."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-direct-set-receiver","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-5","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A direct same-binding call result is established only after the target write; because the overwritten receiver formal is absent from the callee relation, the caller's selected return proves the replacement equality."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-named-outcome-no-publication","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Naming a whole Result before matching it carries no verified selected-payload publication token; the later exact equality requirement therefore remains unproved and FN-8 rejects the guard call."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-value-if-delivery-join","rules":["ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Each value_if edge forwards its deterministic signed-remainder range, the continuation joins the unequal bounds at their weakest common bound, and the exact requirement plus both runtime branches observe the delivered value."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-value-match-no-delivery","rules":["ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A byte-similar value_match carries no forward-delivery relation from its arm-local checked branch facts, so the exact continuation requirement remains unproved and FN-8 rejects."}
{"id": "set2-pos-affine-field-replace", "rules": ["SET-2", "TYPE-5"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SET-2: `let x = replace p = e;` exchanges the affine value at a writable place and binds the previous value as a fresh own binding. The target root stays live and is read again after the commit, and the moved-out value's sole owner is the new binding."}
{"id": "set2-neg-copy-target", "rules": ["SET-2", "OWN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SET-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SET-2 requires the target's final selected type to be affine under OWN-1. A copy-typed target is a hard error citing SET-2 at the complete target place, carrying the restructuring `use set for a copy place; read the previous value bare`."}
{"id": "set2-neg-region-bearing-target", "rules": ["SET-2", "STOR-5"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SET-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SET-2 requires the target's final selected type to be region-free under STOR-5. A slice<'r, U> binding carries a region, so replace on it is a hard error citing SET-2, carrying the restructuring that a slice's static origin set is fixed at initialization. Region-bearing types cannot occur in stored content, so this bites only a direct binding or dereference target."}
{"id": "set2-neg-rhs-type-mismatch", "rules": ["SET-2", "TYPE-5"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "After SET-2 derives a writable affine target place of type T, the right-hand side receives the same exact-`own T` judgment TYPE-5 states, located at the complete expr child of the replace_let_rhs. There is no mode coercion, type conversion, or target-selected overload, so a differently-typed replacement is a hard error citing TYPE-5."}
{"id": "set2-neg-arena-replace-target", "rules": ["SET-2", "STOR-5", "STOR-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SET-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SET-2 requires the target's final selected type to be region-free under STOR-5's relation, and STOR-5 makes a type region-bearing when it contains slice<'r, T> or arena<'r, T> at any depth. An arena descriptor is therefore as region-bearing as a slice, so replacing one arena<'r, u64> with another is a hard error citing SET-2 at the complete target place, carrying the restructuring that a slice's static origin set and an arena's confinement are fixed at initialization."}
{"id": "set2-pos-box-descriptor-replace", "rules": ["SET-2", "STOR-1", "TYPE-5"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The positive control for SET-2's region-bearing rejection: a box<u64> descriptor is affine under OWN-1 and region-free under STOR-5, so replace exchanges the two owning descriptors, binds the previous box as a fresh own binding, and leaves the target root live. Only a region-bearing target type is rejected, not every owning-descriptor target."}
{"id":"op2-pos-overflow-obligation-discharged","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A bare addition with a constant operand carries an OP-2 overflow obligation. A dominating branch establishes the residual upper bound, so the site returns the exact value with no runtime overflow check and the function's effect row is pure."}
{"id": "op2-neg-overflow-obligation-undischarged", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A constant-operand-class call whose fact state does not discharge the obligation is a compile-time rejection citing OP-2 at its infix node, carrying the residual per ENT-6. Repairs include a dominating branch, requirement, invariant, checked source proof, or the explicit wrap, checked, or sat spelling."}
{"id":"op2-pos-wrap-untouched-by-dissolution","rules":["OP-2","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"The arithmetic-mode obligation attaches to the bare constant-operand class only. Wrapping addition is total, carries no obligation, needs no discharge, and contributes nothing to the effect row, so an unbounded operand is valid."}
{"id": "own6-pos-callresult-borrow-chain", "rules": ["OWN-6", "OWN-5", "OWN-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-6 reborrow extension: a callee determining one provenance-candidate parameter makes its borrow-mode result an ordinary borrow holder whose resolved place is the candidate actual's. The child fed to it in candidate position may take a caller-supplied region, composing a grandchild chain of depth two; creating that child suspends the parent for the remainder of its life, so no point sees two usable uniq paths to one place."}
{"id": "fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind-called", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The still-illegal form, now rejected at the boundary rather than at the binding: two uniq parameters in the result's formal region are two provenance candidates, so FN-1 rejects at the callee's complete rtype and OWN-6's binding-side rejection is unreachable. The caller here is retained deliberately, so the case pins that the declaration is still the reported error when a call site exists; fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind pins the same declaration with no caller at all."}
{"id": "const1-pos-forwarded-arithmetic", "rules": ["CONST-1", "FN-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-1 one-operation const arithmetic: a const-expression is at most one operation over two terms. `n * 2` is usable as an array<T, N> size and forwardable as a const targ, and each concrete instantiation evaluates it to one u64 value at monomorphization."}
{"id":"const1-neg-eval-overflow","rules":["CONST-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"CONST-1 evaluates constants exactly in the unsigned 64-bit domain. This multiplication leaves that domain and is rejected at the complete const node; an accepted const expression creates no runtime operation."}
{"id": "const1-neg-runtime-arithmetic-mode", "rules": ["CONST-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "CONST-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-1: the const-expression tail reuses infix_op but its spelling must be one of the five bare operators. Const evaluation has no runtime overflow mode, so a mode-suffixed spelling is a hard error citing CONST-1 at the infix_op node; the grammar admits and the checker restricts."}
{"id": "const2-pos-struct-const", "rules": ["CONST-2", "TYPE-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-2 struct-typed consts: a source struct whose every field type is const-eligible is const-eligible. The cvalue takes the construction form naming its exact struct and writing every declared field in declared order, the const is laid out as one read-only static aggregate, and it is read via field suffixes exactly as subscript reads."}
{"id": "const2-neg-enum-not-const-eligible", "rules": ["CONST-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "CONST-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-2 const-eligibility is a closed list of primitives, arrays of const-eligible T, and source structs of const-eligible fields. Enums are not const-eligible and stay DEFERRED with recorded delta: a payload-enum const has no non-consuming read path, and a tag-only-enum const needs a constant-value family no current program demands."}
{"id":"op2-pos-division-obligation-discharged","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unsigned bare division carries the two-conjunct OP-2 domain obligation. A dominating branch establishes a nonzero divisor, both conjuncts discharge, and the accepted site keeps no runtime zero-divisor check, so the function is pure."}
{"id":"op2-neg-division-obligation-undischarged","rules":["OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same unsigned division with nothing proving the divisor nonzero rejects at its infix node with residual d != 0. A missing proof never creates a runtime fallback."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-requires-nonzero", "rules": ["OP-2", "FN-8", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A nonzero function requirement is proved at the call site and discharges the callee's exact division obligation. The closed program observes 84 / 2 as 42."}
{"id": "op2-pos-unsigned-literal-division-images", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-3", "FN-8", "FN-9", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Unsigned division by the positive literal 2 publishes quotient <= count and 2 * quotient <= count for the exact runtime values. The first image proves one callee requirement; the scaled image admits the exact multiplication, proves the other requirement and the postcondition, and the closed call observes 2 * floor(9 / 2) as 8."}
{"id": "op2-neg-division-constant-zero-divisor", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An integer-typed named const reads as an ENT-2 constant, so the site is in the divisor class, and its zero divisor is a ground false conjunct rejected at every non-contradictory point. The always-invalid bare spelling is never accepted."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-constant-divisor-total", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A nonzero constant divisor that is not -1 discharges both conjuncts with no fact source at all, so a signed n / 2_i32 is total and pure and emits one plain instruction."}
{"id": "op2-neg-division-minus-one-divisor-unbounded", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The one expressible signed-overflow shape: n / -1_i32 leaves the second conjunct as the residual `n != -2147483648` and rejects at the infix node."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-minus-one-divisor-bounded", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The same -1 divisor with a dominating branch bounding the dividend above i32::MIN discharges both conjuncts and keeps no runtime test."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-remainder-same-obligation", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "% carries the identical obligation as / and discharges from the identical fact: one obligation family covers both spellings."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-checked-untouched", "rules": ["OP-2", "OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The dissolution attaches obligations to the bare divisor class only. /checked is untouched: it needs no discharge, stays pure, and returns Err(DivideByZero()) for a zero divisor."}
{"id":"eff2-pos-discharged-division-site-pure-row","rules":["EFF-2","OP-2","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"The exact division domain is discharged statically [EFF-2, OP-2, ENT-6], so the operation has no runtime fallback and pure is the exact effect row."}
{"id": "fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Two same-kind same-region parameters are two provenance candidates, so FN-1 rejects at the complete rtype with no caller anywhere in the program. This is the case fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind-called cannot carry: it proves the declaration alone is the error."}
{"id": "fn1-neg-result-provenance-other-kind", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Exactly one parameter is a candidate for the shared-borrow result, but another names the result's formal region in the other borrow kind. FN-1 rejects for that reason alone: candidacy is written-mode identity, not a count."}
{"id": "fn1-pos-result-provenance-distinct-regions", "rules": ["FN-1", "OWN-6", "SET-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "FN-1's own restructuring applied: the source parameter gets its own region, so exactly one parameter shares the result's region and kind. The caller binds the result holder, writes through it, and the write reaches the place the debtor parameter was borrowed from."}
{"id": "fn1-pos-result-provenance-zero-candidate", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The zero-candidate arm, on the contract-member surface where signature formation is the whole judgment. No parameter is a candidate, which is legal by elimination: OWN-10 admits no result-region borrow rooted in callee-local storage, so the only remaining source is named const storage."}
{"id": "sys14-list-outcome-exhaustive", "rules": ["SYS-6", "SYS-14", "ERR-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "612e747874", "bytes": "78"}, {"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ListOutcome is closed over ListBytes carrying an absolute portable-prefix endpoint and entry count, ListEnd, and ListFailed. A match naming all three is exhaustive; this arrangement reaches ListBytes and exits 0."}
{"id":"reject-sys14-list-end-beyond-buffer","rules":["SYS-8","SYS-14","ERR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"SYS-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"directory_next carries SYS-8's two static range goals. End 4104 exceeds the 4096-byte destination, so compilation rejects the call before any host enumeration or runtime fallback."}
{"id": "sys14-list-zero-range", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "612e747874", "bytes": "78"}, {"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A zero-length destination range transfers nothing and yields ListBytes(next: start, entries: 0), never ListEnd. This case starts at zero, so the observed endpoint remains zero."}
{"id": "sys14-open-directory-component", "rules": ["SYS-14", "SYS-7", "PATH-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "open_directory validates its caller-owned name range before any host call. A range containing a target separator is not one path component, so it yields InvalidPath with both detail fields zero, no host call, and no DirectoryRead result; the consumed FilePermit is burned on that failure."}
{"id": "sys14-open-directory-empty-name", "rules": ["SYS-14", "SYS-7"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An empty name range names no component and yields InvalidPath with both detail fields zero. The buffer holds a valid component and only the requested range is empty, which makes this the range judgment rather than a content judgment."}
{"id": "sys14-open-directory-success", "rules": ["SYS-10", "SYS-14", "SYS-7"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "open_directory consumes one explicit FilePermit, reads the shared invocation DirectoryRead selector, and returns a fresh ordinary DirectoryRead for the arranged component. Exit 0 is reached only through Ok, so a build that answered every open with a validation failure would not pass."}
{"id": "sys14-list-handle-affine", "rules": ["OWN-1", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A DirectorySource is an ordinary affine owner. Moving it ends the source binding's ownership, so a later exclusive borrow of the moved-from name is rejected by ordinary ownership before traversal semantics is consulted."}
{"id": "sys14-list-handle-unique", "rules": ["OWN-5", "SYS-14", "SYS-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "directory_next advances DirectorySource through an exclusive borrow. This case deliberately forms two simultaneous unique borrows of the same owner, so the existing OWN-5 overlap rule rejects them without an Ordered relation or system-specific permission."}
{"id": "sys14-no-path-from-bytes", "rules": ["TYPE-5", "PATH-1", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An entry name reaches source only as bytes: no operation turns an enumerated name into a HostString or a RelativePath, because a name's backing is not the command-lifetime argument snapshot while a path value is an inline lease over it. The gap is closed by the argument type at relative_path, not by convention."}
{"id": "sys14-directory-release", "rules": ["SYS-5", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "612e747874", "bytes": "78"}, {"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "DirectorySource is release-complete: the value bound in the Ok arm leaves scope there and compiler-derived release is the complete obligation, so no source close or finish operation exists. The enclosing DirectoryRead release contributes the command's exact writes(cwd) row."}
{"id": "sys14-entry-kind-closed", "rules": ["SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "612e747874", "bytes": "78"}, {"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "One portable entry record is one kind byte, one little-endian u16 name length, and exactly that many name bytes. The kind set is closed at 0 through 4, names are nonempty, and ListBytes reports the exact number of complete records in its portable prefix [SYS-14]."}
{"id": "accept-sysfile-two-permits-shared-directory", "rules": ["OWN-1", "OWN-5", "EFF-5", "SYS-10", "PAR-1"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Two short unique loans of one FileFactory mint two independent affine FilePermit owners. After reservation returns, two opens may consume those permits through shared loans of one DirectoryRead; neither operation retains a factory loan or requires an exclusive directory loan."}
{"id": "reject-sysfile-permit-used-twice", "rules": ["OWN-1", "SYS-10"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "FilePermit is an ordinary affine value. One open consumes it on every outcome, so a second move of the same permit is rejected by the existing use-after-move rule with no I/O-specific identity machinery."}
{"id": "v033-neg-missing-result-binding", "rules": ["FORM-3"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Every function result is writer-named at the declaration boundary. A bare result type after `->` is not an alternate anonymous form, so parsing rejects this declaration at that type before any body judgment."}
{"id": "v033-pos-shared-contract-define", "rules": ["FN-8", "FN-9"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "One erased, pure contract definition is visible to both a requirement and an ensured normal-return relation. It is alpha-expanded independently at the caller and return edge, creates no runtime storage, and is not visible in the function body."}
{"id": "v033-pos-uninhabited-contract", "rules": ["FN-8", "ENT-4", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Contradictory independent requirements make this concrete function legally uninhabited. The contradictory entry state discharges its deliberately impossible subscript without making the function callable from a reachable state; ordinary IR tests separately pin unreachable lowering."}
{"id": "v033-neg-exact-domain-unproved", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A two-variable exact addition has one IntegerDomain obligation whose canonical predicate is `left +defined right`. With no dominating checked fact or source proof, the operation is rejected statically; exact arithmetic has no implicit runtime-overflow fallback."}
{"id": "s7-pos-signed-remainder-ranges-run", "rules": ["ENT-3", "ENT-6", "OP-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Signed exact remainders by fixed nonzero divisors publish their deterministic result intervals. The affine domain checker combines those intervals to prove the following exact addition, which computes 42."}
{"id":"v033-neg-allocation-fit-unproved","rules":["OP-9","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A runtime buffer length with no established buffer_fits relation leaves AllocationFit unproved. OP-9 rejects the constructor at its source node before lowering and creates no runtime size check."}
{"id": "v033-run-system-nonzero-next", "rules": ["SYS-8"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "4142"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "host_copy_bytes copies the arranged two-byte argument into the proved nonzero range `[1,3)` and must return the absolute success endpoint 3. A legacy transferred-count interpretation would return 2; the program also verifies the untouched prefix and copied bytes."}
{"id": "v033-run-open-file-regular", "rules": ["SYS-7", "SYS-8", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "6974656d", "bytes": "78"}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Opening the arranged regular component `item` through DirectoryRead validates the proved range and returns Ok(ReadFile). Backend target tests separately pin no-follow flags and descriptor inspection."}
{"id": "v033-run-open-file-directory", "rules": ["SYS-7", "SYS-8", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "6974656d", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "When the arranged component `item` is a directory, open_file returns exactly `IsDirectory(code: 0, origin: 0)`; only that constructor and detail pair reaches exit 0. Ordinary backend evidence pins provisional cleanup shape."}
{"id": "accept-par3-staged-iteration-own-scratch", "rules": ["PAR-3", "PAR-1", "SYS-10", "OWN-5", "EFF-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A counted loop whose body constructs the name and destination it uses. The only storage outside the loop it reaches is the factory it reserves through, the directory it reads, and the total it commits, so every place carries a PAR-3 disposition: read-only for the directory, prologue-serialized for the factory whose unique loan reserve_file takes and returns inline [SYS-10], remainder-serialized for the total, and replicated for the two per-iteration buffers. Acceptance is unchanged by the permission."}
{"id": "accept-par3-staged-loop-with-prologue-break", "rules": ["PAR-3", "GRAM-4", "SYS-10", "OWN-5"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An uncounted loop whose exit and whose carried index are both written before the submission. PAR-3's unit is the iteration the statement graph gives rather than an index subrange, so a loop_stmt is admitted on the same terms as a for_stmt and an early typed exit written in the prologue is admitted; the index the body advances is reached only before the cut, so the prologue serializes it."}
{"id": "accept-par3-staged-denied-hoisted-scratch", "rules": ["PAR-3", "SYS-8", "OWN-5"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The destination is hoisted above the loop and the fold reads its whole capacity rather than the prefix the transfer defined [SYS-8], so after a short read the bytes beyond it are the previous iteration's and the published total is order-dependent. The program is accepted, as every program under a permission rule is; its staged verdict is a denial naming the hoisted destination, and a whole-place write-before-read rule that admitted it would silently publish different bytes."}
{"id": "accept-par3-staged-denied-read-before-write", "rules": ["PAR-3", "SYS-8", "OWN-5"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The fold of the hoisted destination is written before the transfer that fills it, so on every path through the body the read of that storage precedes any write of it in the same iteration. This is a true loop-carried dependence that no coverage proof can ever admit, kept beside the previous case so the two shapes stay distinguishable as the analysis behind the replicated disposition grows."}
{"id": "accept-par3-staged-denied-carried-scratch-byte", "rules": ["PAR-3", "OWN-5", "EFF-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The marker byte one iteration writes into the hoisted scratch survives into the next, because the callee writes it only on odd indices and so has no written extent its signature fixes. Executing prologues in index order would preserve that carried byte, but the remainder reads the same storage, so no single segment serializes it and the staged verdict is a denial naming the scratch."}
{"id": "accept-par3-staged-denied-opaque-cursor", "rules": ["PAR-3", "SYS-14", "OWN-5"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The enumeration source is a cursor with one position, opened above the loop and advanced by every iteration through a retained exclusive borrow [SYS-14]. It is not read-only, it is not prologue-only, and an opaque system nominal has no copy element type, so no replication can ever reach it. This denial is the ownership model working rather than a limit of this version."}
{"id": "accept-par3-staged-denied-exit-in-remainder", "rules": ["PAR-3", "FN-1", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The body returns on its first failed open, and that edge lies after the submission the body is cut at. With later iterations already in flight, the decision to leave would be taken after opens the source-order execution never performs, and an open is an externally observable transition that is not rolled back, so the staged verdict is a denial by the exit condition."}
{"id": "gram5-pos-comparison-operators", "rules": ["GRAM-5", "OP-1", "OP-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The six integer comparisons are `compare_op` infix expressions over two atoms, each an integer-only table row, and a call writes its type arguments after the `::` delimiter; every comparison holds on the ordered pair and the program exits 0."}
{"id": "gram5-neg-type-application-without-delimiter", "rules": ["GRAM-5", "DIAG-1", "FORM-3"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A call's type arguments are written after the `::` delimiter. Spelled bare, `cvt<u8, u32>(narrow)` begins the comparison `cvt < u8` [GRAM-5], and the derivation fails at `u8` where a comparison operand atom must stand; DIAG-1 row 3 attributes that keyword, which has IDENT's raw shape and fails only its fixed-atom exclusion, to FORM-3."}
{"id": "gram4-neg-multiplied-use-relation-bare", "rules": ["GRAM-4", "PRF-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "GRAM-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A relation premise is delimited. Written bare, `use 3 times first + second <= ...` does not derive: after the multiplicity the grammar admits a named invariant or a delimited relation and nothing else."}
{"id": "prf1-pos-multiplied-relation-use", "rules": ["PRF-1", "ENT-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A certificate scales a relation-form premise by an explicit factor of three, `use 3 times (first + second <= first_limit + second_limit)`; the parenthesized relation is itself proved by AUTO from the two requirements and the scaled sum closes the target."}
{"id": "inv1-neg-equality-relation", "rules": ["INV-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "INV-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An invariant relation is one of the four ordered comparisons; `==` in relation position is outside the invariant surface and is rejected citing INV-1 at the `compare_op` node."}
{"id": "form8-pos-elided-regions-run", "rules": ["FORM-8", "OWN-3", "OWN-4", "FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Every elided region spelling composes and runs: a declaration whose only region relates to nothing writes no region parameter list, an unnamed `region` block introduces the region an unnamed borrow takes, and the call writes no `::` type application because the callee's parameter position determines its region [FORM-8]."}
{"id": "form8-neg-written-unrelated-parameter-region", "rules": ["FORM-8"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A parameter writes a region name no other position of its declaration names. [FORM-8] writes a region name only to relate two positions or to name an output-position region the caller chooses, so this position is unnamed."}
{"id": "form8-neg-written-innermost-borrow-region", "rules": ["FORM-8"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A borrow writes the region of the `region` block that most closely encloses it. [FORM-8] writes a borrow's region only when it is not that block's region."}
{"id": "form8-neg-unreferenced-region-block-name", "rules": ["FORM-8"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A `region` block writes a name its body never references after elision. [FORM-8] writes the block's name exactly when a use inside it survives."}
{"id": "form8-neg-written-determined-region-argument", "rules": ["FORM-8", "FN-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A call writes a region argument for a callee region parameter that occupies a parameter position. [FORM-8] writes exactly the region parameters no parameter position determines; this call's own argument determines this one."}
{"id": "form8-neg-elided-result-region", "rules": ["FORM-8", "FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A borrow-mode result elides its region. Every output position writes its region under [FORM-8]: either the result shares a parameter's region, or no parameter determines it and the caller supplies it."}
{"id": "form8-pos-related-pair-written", "rules": ["FORM-8", "FN-1", "OWN-4"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The one relation [FORM-8] keeps writable: the result borrows the parameter, the same region is meant at two positions, and the name is written at both with the region parameter list holding exactly it. The caller still writes no region argument, because the parameter position determines it."}
{"id": "form8-neg-elided-related-region", "rules": ["FORM-8", "OWN-4", "FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Eliding one half of a related pair does not relate the positions: the parameter's unwritten region is a region of its own, and a borrow in it does not outlive the written result region [OWN-4]. The relation exists only where [FORM-8] writes the name at both positions."}
{"id": "own11-pos-loop-body-region", "rules": ["OWN-11", "FORM-8"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-11 positive: a loop body is itself a region block, so a borrow written directly in the body takes the body's own per-iteration region and is written bare with no `region` block at all. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "form8-neg-region-block-is-the-loop-body", "rules": ["FORM-8", "OWN-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A `region` block is the loop body's only statement, so its block is exactly that body and the body already introduces one region over the same block [OWN-11]. [FORM-8] admits one spelling of one region, so the block is rejected and its statements become the loop body."}
{"id": "form8-pos-narrower-loop-region-block", "rules": ["FORM-8", "OWN-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A named `region` block inside a loop the body writes another statement beside is strictly narrower than the iteration, is not a second spelling of the body's own region, and stays legal; its name is written because a borrow inside a nested block still references it [FORM-8]. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "ent6-pos-nested-join-keeps-the-flat-image", "rules": ["ENT-6", "INV-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ENT-6 positive: a three-way demux written as nested conditionals passes through two joins in sequence. Each input image is normalized by folding the earlier join's delta atom back into its constant interval, so the second join reaches the image one flat join reaches and every per-iteration header invariant is preserved. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "ent6-pos-join-shape-independence", "rules": ["ENT-6", "INV-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ENT-6 positive pair: the same four-lane branch set written as one flat `match` and as three-level nested conditionals reaches the same joined image, so acceptance does not depend on the shape of the control join. Runs to exit 0."}
{"id": "ent6-neg-nested-join-does-not-invent-a-bound", "rules": ["ENT-6", "INV-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "INV-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ENT-6 negative: one nested arm advances the accumulator by two, so no per-iteration bound of one is preserved on every path. Folding an earlier join's delta into its interval widens nothing, and the backedge obligation is still refused citing INV-1."}
{"id": "msr5-pos-two-measure-clause", "rules": ["MSR-5", "GRAM-5", "FN-8", "ENT-2", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A requires clause names a measure of a place directly on both sides of one comparison and again against a literal, with no contract definition in the block. Before MSR-5 the same fact needed one define per measure and the direct clause was a GRAM-5 parse rejection at the comparison."}
{"id": "msr3-pos-own-operand-call-datum", "rules": ["MSR-3", "CALL-6", "ENT-3", "FN-9", "MSR-5"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An ensures names the measure of an own parameter the call consumes. The operand denotes that call's call datum, a compiler-owned term with empty support, so the published relation survives the consume the same statement performs and the caller discharges a later requirement from it."}
{"id": "msr3-neg-uniq-state-measure-in-ensures", "rules": ["MSR-3", "FN-9"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "MSR-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A source-declared ensures names a measure of a &uniq state parameter. That parameter is the one position from which a callee could leave a caller holding a measure of a value the callee replaced, so the operand is inadmissible and the clause is refused with the take-it-by-value restructuring."}
{"id": "call6-neg-contradictory-published-relations", "rules": ["CALL-6", "FN-9", "ENT-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "CALL-6"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Two ensures clauses publish relations that cannot hold together. At a contradictory point every relation and both signs of every goal are derivable, so an inconsistent contract is not one wrong fact at one caller but every fact at every caller; the set is fixed at the declaration and refused there."}
{"id": "call6-pos-routed-relation-over-a-call-datum", "rules": ["CALL-6", "MSR-3", "FN-9", "ENT-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A variant-routed ensures names a consumed own operand's measure. The relation is instantiated at the call over that call's call datum and established at the selected arm, where the caller reads it to discharge a later requirement."}
{"id": "call4-neg-measured-result-not-admitted", "rules": ["CALL-4", "FN-9", "MSR-5"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-9"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An ensures names a measure of a result of measured type. MSR-5 makes the clause parse, and CALL-4 records that a result of measured type and a measure over a result place are deferred, so the ordinary FN-9 result-datum admission refuses it. This pins that the refusal is a semantic admission and no longer a grammar rejection."}
{"id": "prf1-pos-term-multiplicity", "rules": ["PRF-1", "ENT-6", "ENT-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A matrix inner index at a runtime stride. `use n times (p < k);` scales one premise by an unsigned value, which makes the accumulated sum a degree-two polynomial; its `n*p` and `n*k` monomials fold to the value images the two admitted exact multiplications bound, and the residual against the target is then the constant zero."}
{"id": "prf1-neg-signed-multiplicity", "rules": ["PRF-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRF-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The same certificate over signed operands. A named multiplicity must be unsigned, because nonnegativity is what makes scaling a normalized premise sound and a signed value could reverse it; the type is the whole check, so nothing here becomes an obligation."}
{"id": "prf1-neg-unfolded-nonlinear-sum", "rules": ["PRF-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRF-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A term-scaled premise whose `n*p` monomial no admitted multiplication in scope equals. The accumulated sum never reduces to an affine inequality, so PRF-1 rejects rather than measuring the target against a weaker sum."}
{"id": "gram4-neg-bare-relation-premise", "rules": ["GRAM-4", "PRF-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "GRAM-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A relation premise is delimited whether or not it carries a multiplicity. Written bare, `use x <= middle;` does not derive: after `use` the grammar admits an invariant name, a delimited relation, or a multiplicity, and an affine relation is none of those."}
{"id": "prf1-pos-term-multiplicity-shared-product", "rules": ["PRF-1", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Two bindings hold the same product `n*p`. Both equal the monomial the term-scaled premise passes through, so either folds soundly; the one the target itself names is chosen, because a monomial folded to an equal value under another name would not cancel."}
{"id": "gram4-neg-typed-multiplicity", "rules": ["GRAM-4", "PRF-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "GRAM-4"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A multiplicity is a bare decimal, so a suffixed literal does not derive. The citation is the position's own rule: `const` and this position share the grammar's sole pattern predicate, and only the first is CONST-1's."}
{"id": "prf1-pos-binder-multiplicity", "rules": ["PRF-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A counted loop binder is a named multiplicity. PRF-1 admits any live own-mode unsigned integer value binding there, not only a let-bound local or a parameter, and a counted binder is own u64 by the language's own rule."}
